          'A function can access names in its local scope and enclosing scopes, following Python’s LEGB lookup rule: local, enclosing, global, then built-in.',
          'Assignments inside a function create local names by default. Use parameters to pass dependencies in; global and nonlocal should be used sparingly because they introduce shared state.',
          'course = "Python"\n\ndef announce():\n    message = f"Learning {course}"\n    return message\n\nprint(announce())',
          'announce reads the module-level course because it does not assign to that name. A nested function can also retain access to a value from its enclosing function.',
