        7: guidedLecture('Lecture 7: Asynchronous Iteration and Best Practices',
          'Asynchronous iterables produce values that may require waiting, such as stream chunks. Python consumes them with async for inside an async function.',
          'An asynchronous iterator defines __aiter__ and __anext__. Use async iteration only when the source is asynchronous; ordinary collections should use standard for loops.',
          'async def read_chunks(stream):\n    async for chunk in stream:\n        process(chunk)',
          'The loop waits for each chunk from the asynchronous source. Error handling can be added around the loop when reads or processing may fail.',
          'Use normal iterators for synchronous data and async iterators for values that arrive asynchronously. Keep consumption bounded when a source may be unending.'),
